at formulation and we feel that it  will be successful  we'll go ahead and freeze that  formulation and  commercialize it however before  after kind of after the the products  frozen or we've got a good  idea of what that product is going to be  we've gone through some tests  we will offer our products for testing  to other  inoculant manufacturers for testing  prior to commercialization and if we  come back and there's some big issues  we will uh withhold that  product from the marketplace currently  the only company that's really been  taking us  up on this offer is novazymes so i'm  going to be sharing some  information from novazymes but we do  they do test the compatibility of all  our new formulations and  treatments with with their inoculant  products and  it's something that syngenta doesn't  really have the expertise to do in-house  so we have  the inoculant companies are really good  at testing those things  and we have them them do that and when  they give us back the information  and then they publish it in a guide so  you can download  this guide you know i did last week from  from novazymes and it's a compatibility  guide and it gives you  by crop all of the different products  that they have i've just taken a snip  of one of them which is tag team pete  and then it lists all of the  products that have been  tested by syngenta or i'm  tested by novazones i'm sorry and you  can see there's a  pretty big list of those products here  and i just want to highlight a few  this one is a apron max rfc the rfc  actually stood for rhizobium friendly  constant concentrate we did make some  modifications to the formulation  to help it be more uh friendly to  to rhizobium actually than the original  aprimax rta formulation and you can see  that it did  seem to improve the planting window and  this planting window is actually  the maximum amount of time that can  elapse between the date the seed was  treated with the bio  bioenhancer or rhizobium  and when it can be