ood storage practices  before and after the application  you want to make sure that there's cool  temperatures that favor  the survival of the bacteria found in  the inoculants  store the products and seed to which the  products have been applied in a cool dry  place  and refer to the conditions  on the product and seed labels for  specific recommendations  on how to best keep those organisms  happy  the other thing is you can have a  dedicated tank  and for the inoculum and a pumping  system this will allow for easy  management of the inocument or  biological products  and while the standard seed treatments  are generally stable  if left in a mixed tank for a few days  before consumed it's not true for  inoculants  a dedicated tank would allow you to open  and mix  only what you need for immediate use and  keeping your  inoculant source as fresh and as viable  as possible  and even with the dedicated tank need to  apply all  of the product within 24 hours after  opening  and if you don't have a dedicated tank  you can  choose to mix the inoculants with the  treatment but if this  is done that like i said you need to be  using all of that slurry with up within  four to six hours  and another important thing is to clean  out your equipment  and make sure that you have good  sanitation throughout so that the  sea treatment and the and the inoculant  that you use in the next  next batch are also compatible or make  sure things are clean  another thing that a lot of people don't  really think about is the water  so you may be treating with a city water  and as we know they treat city water  with  with chlorine to help kill microbes in  the water so  this is not going to be compatible with  your inoculum  and so if you're treating with the city  water which has chlorine added  you may want to install a water filter  which is designed to remove the  chlorine or use a spare tank where you  can  hold the water for 24 hours and allow  the chlorine to dissipate before you  use it in your slurry also many treaters  use  soybean oil to help or other oils to  help  with the treatment process and generally  this has been shown not to be an issue  and doesn't seem to have any effect or  harm on the  on the bacteria and it also may even  improve the  quality a little bit because it dries a  little bit slower than just  straight water it's also other  important to check all of the other  things that you're adding to the  the mixture the slurry in in addition to  the seed treatment part you can add  other polymers or colors or maybe  another product  uh from somewhere and you want to make  sure you check all of those when you're  applying it with a with an inoculant  so important to think about all of the  things that are going into your slurry  i've talked a lot about the inoculants i  talked about the  seed treatments and now another growing  area is really a biological  so a lot of these biologicals could be  living organisms as well what are really  growers looking for  for a biological we're trying to unlock  soil-bound nutrients we're trying  to enhance access to those nutrients fix  atmospheric nitrogen stimulate  development or protect  against pathogens so  and some of these can be live organisms  or  plant extracts small peptides  all kinds of things that you want to  make sure  those are the integrity of those is  maintained when you're mixing them  for bio control usually these can be it  could be viruses  bacterial fungal pathogens pheromones or  botanicals or other substances  in biostimulants you could have  microbial products  nitrogen fixing bacteria algal extracts  humic acids amino acids peptides enzymes  vitamins  all kinds of things nutrients are  usually  micro or macro nutrients that generally  are not  considered incompatible with seed  treatments  in a living sense but could be  incompatible  from a physical chemistry type of sense  so if the biological or the organism is  actually a living organism  the same kind of rules would apply as  we've talked about for the rhizobium  bacteria  you want to make sure that those are  happy and those conditions for that  organism  are satisfied and want to test and make  sure that those  bacteria are still alive on the on the  seed after  after application that it's compatible  and the testing for this compatibility  is done by a lot of the biological  companies that are bringing these new  products to the market  we're often approached by companies that  ask for product to test  when they're starting out trying to  determine if they can  apply a product to the seed and if that  would be a mechanism  of delivery for their product or  potentially they would have to go in  furrow  and do a application that way instead of  applying it with the seed treatment  they're also trying to figure out  whether they can do the tank mix or it  would be built  it could be built into a formulation a c  treatment formulation  or if it's better doing a sequential  application something like that  and so this there's a lot of work that's  been done in this  this area but if you're not sure about  the products you're using  or if they've been tested it's always  good to ask  you know ask the manufacturer what what  the compatibility is  and what kind of testing's been done we  do syngenta  doesn't do a lot of compatibility  testing  of all the products the biological  products on the market unless we have an  agreement  with that company that we're interested  in they're biological  or they've asked us to test it or  have to supply some product for them to  test but there is  testing going on  so i did mention uh you know just  a lot about the biologicals that were  thinking about  and how they're compatible trying to  keep them alive  but there's also physical compatibility  and some of these other products that i  mentioned  uh humic acids the  they're not they're not living but  they're products that are being applied  on the seed as biological and  it's important to look at the  compatibility of those if you're going  to apply those at the same time or put  them into your slurry  and one of the best ways to do that is  to do a jar test  and you can learn a lot from a jar test  that'll save you a lot of trouble  cleaning out lines and tanks and  cleaning up the mess later  we do tests with our products with  biologicals that we are thinking about  or we know that may be  put into a into a tank mix  and one of the things we're looking for  is the incompatibility just physical  incompatibility  uh change in viscosity change in  appearance or  physical behavior or separation like you  can see here in this  this jar and that's something you want  to try  to avoid sometimes there's a little bit  of separation but if it's recirculated  easily  then we usually say that that would be  acceptable  and there's also compatibilities where  we increase the temperature  decrease the temperature make sure that  in a range of temperatures it's okay  so there's a lot of tests that are done  with just physical compatibility  also it's very important to clean the  equipment when changing formulation  types  just a good cleanliness really helps out  with  seed treating and making sure that  everything is is  compatible and flowing easily and  another  one way to clean out easily is using a  simple green something like that  so i did go through quickly quite a lot  of  information and i just wanted to  talk a little bit about some of the  sources of the information i have  there's the bio  nova zones bio ag c treatment  compatibility guide  this i did take some information from  the 2013 one but there is another one  that  was updated more recently and there's a  best practices guide that's available  online  from this january there was some  information you can find  from compatibility of different  inoculants from  from bsf in canada there's a  syngenta technical bulletins or  technical bulletins from your company  or other c treatment companies about  the inoculum compatibility or biological  compatibilities  and then there's different papers that  you can read about online  quite a lot of journal articles and  other information that you can you can  go to to  really dig into some of this as well so  if  with that if if there's any time left  i'd like to  you know get take some questions  david thank you very much for the for  the talk yes just  a a question or so it's again packing a  lot of information in a short amount of  time  and again yes also again the cca credits  there's your information to go ahead and  get those cca credits  so a question for david on compatibility  of the seed treatment  chemical seed treatments with the  rhizobia or the bio controls  okay and i don't see anything in the  chat right now okay  david again thank you very much thank  you very much for this great  presentation  you're welcome  this has been a production of cornell  university  on the web at cornell.edu


